   "text": "The drawing shows zidovudine (AZT), a thymine nucleoside analogue.\n\n- **Base:** a thymine ring with a methyl on the ring carbon, two C=O groups and one N\u2013H. Its N1 is attached to the sugar.\n- **Sugar:** a five-membered ring made of one oxygen and four carbons. The carbon next to the ring oxygen that bears CH2OH has a wedge bond. The anomeric carbon bonded to the thymine nitrogen also has a wedge bond. The ring CH2 sits between the anomeric carbon and the azide-bearing carbon.\n- **Azide:** N=[N+]=[N-] on the ring carbon drawn with a hashed bond. This is the 3\u2032 position, so the 3\u2032-OH is replaced by azide.\n- **Stereochemistry:** the base and CH2OH are both wedged, so they are on the same face (\u03b2), and the azide is hashed on the opposite face. This is the natural D-configuration pattern and matches the known stereo-SMILES for AZT, which I used.\n\nSMILES: CC1=CN(C(=O)NC1=O)[C@H]2C[C@@H]([C@H](O2)CO)N=[N+]=[N-]"
